The QASPER dataset is a scientific question-answering dataset designed to evaluate systems that answer questions about research papers. It contains questions written by readers of scientific articles, along with reference answers grounded in the content of those papers. The dataset supports research on information retrieval, evidence-based question answering, and natural language processing.

In this project, QASPER is used to evaluate a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system and a proposed Adaptive RAG approach. The validation split initially contained 281 paper records, which were processed into 14,108 text chunks. For the experiment, a fixed sample of 200 questions from 83 unique research papers was selected. Each question record contains the question text, paper identifier, question identifier, and reference answer or answers.

The research papers were divided into smaller text passages to make relevant information easier to retrieve. These passages were indexed using dense embeddings generated by a SentenceTransformer model and lexical retrieval using BM25. The two retrieval rankings were combined using Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF). Paper-aware filtering ensured that each question retrieved passages from its corresponding research paper.

The dataset was used to compare three configurations: standard RAG retrieving the top five passages, standard RAG retrieving the top eight passages, and the proposed evidence-preserving Adaptive RAG method. The adaptive method attempts to retrieve additional passages when the initial evidence-confidence score falls below a threshold, while preserving the original retrieved passages.

Token-level F1 score was used to compare predicted answers with the reference answers. The same 200 questions were used for all configurations to support a fair comparison. The experiment produced mean F1 scores of 0.133403 for top-5 RAG, 0.134522 for top-8 RAG, and 0.135355 for Adaptive RAG.

QASPER is suitable for this project because it focuses on answering questions using scientific literature rather than relying solely on general knowledge. It enables the evaluation of retrieval strategies and provides a basis for investigating whether adaptive evidence retrieval can improve scientific question-answering performance.
